    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4886B\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the penal law, in relation to offenses relating to\nunauthorized recordings\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo upgrade New York's piracy laws to include new recoding technologies.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 6 of section 275.00 of the penal law,\nas added by chapter 460 of the laws of 1990, is amended and a new\nsubdivision 7 is added.\n\nSection two amends penal law 275.00.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York has a serious problem with the crime of music piracy. Street\nvendors and retail locations sell fraudulent sound recordings openly\nthroughout the state and are supplied by large scale underground\nmanufacturing and distribution operations. Music piracy is a serious\neconomic crime that deprives artists and record labels of hard earned\nprofits, resulting in losses of jobs in New York and across the\nnation. Further, legitimate music retailers throughout the state are\nforced to compete with music pirates that undercut their businesses\nby failing to pay for music, and failing to pay local, state, and\nfederal taxes.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nFirst of November next succeeding the date on which it shall have\nbecome law.\n\n",
